    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-14982",
      "problem": "Fill in the blanks so that the following reasoning is correct:\n\nThe notation $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/fb6fbc91b2218218acab5a0fe1baed797fc7ece0800c3280bc0d53af6d765ae3.svg) It is equivalent to the statement that for $\\[6pt]$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/48442365817dc8d3a14cc2f5931284b07e8cbfccf0bd2a097f7362f0f97348cb.svg)\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/20865eee0320e3c8d4822421723a5a251048e7158888ac6416427f632bcb85b7.svg) we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/45453872ffd37b70f166312cf1aa4c3966437e91dc293ac0db198d895e41821c.svg)",
      "worked_solution": "The notation $a \\not\\mid b$ means $a$ does not divide $b.$ So, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/assets/833ccbc866a4951df8fa798f0780922c3dc56bff0b2baf1a36711c3f47c9f313.svg)\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb \\neq a k.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ is equivalent to the statement that for $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{ any}}$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{integer}}$ $k,$ we have $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 \\neq 5k}.$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114596/repairs/completeness-3160fa2c4e896719/source/26d33f1e9a0460fe296d0f25a26c7496c353689b3655f35559a3479df80d89b5.html",
